00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.178
Hello, I'm Serzen Patel.

2
00:00:03.178 --> 00:00:08.739
In this video, we will be showing

3
00:00:08.739 --> 00:00:14.300
you how to use a binocular indirect

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.387
ophthalmoscope or BIO.

5
00:00:15.387 --> 00:00:19.735
The main use of a BIO is to perform a fundus examination.

6
00:00:19.735 --> 00:00:20.460
It allows

7
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.043
the user to see a wide stereoscopic field of view or 3D view of

8
00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:26.680
the back of the eye.

9
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.814
When scleral indentation is used, you can also get quick and dynamic views of

10
00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:31.700
the far periphery

11
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.708
of the retina.

12
00:00:32.708 --> 00:00:36.740
This is important in patients complaining of flashes or floaters who may

13
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:41.540
have developed a retinal tear as well as premature babies for judging the stage of retinopathy

14
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.540
or prematurity.

15
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.056
Another big benefit of the BIO is that it allows a good view of

16
00:00:47.056 --> 00:00:48.060
the retina despite immediate

17
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.460
opacity such as corneal scarring, cataract or even blood in the vitreous.

18
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.620
It is also mobile and easy to carry around.

19
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.060
It can therefore be used to examine patients with poor mobility such as patients who are

20
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.420
wheelchair bound or bed bound.

21
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.660
For less cooperative patients such as babies and young children, you can usually still get

22
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.241
good idea of the back of the eye if it's healthy or not with a brief view.

23
00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:10.780
Place the

24
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.327
BIO on your head making sure it's comfortable and secure so that it does

25
00:01:14.327 --> 00:01:15.340
not move when you're

26
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:15.760
examining.

27
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.700
You can see us adjusting it here.

28
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.789
Turn the light on and check that the beam is aligned with your line of sight.

29
00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:23.300
So you're

30
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.567
able to see your thumb singly at arm's length and it's in the centre

31
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:27.500
of each eye's field

32
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.460
of view.

33
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.820
Adjust the brightness to suit the examination

34
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:32.805
you're about to perform.

35
00:01:32.805 --> 00:01:35.020
In babies, it's best to start with a low

36
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.940
illumination, then gradually increase until you can see enough detail

37
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.700
without causing disturbance to the child.

38
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.460
In adults, start with a medium

39
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.511
brightness and spot size.

40
00:01:43.511 --> 00:01:46.140
You need to use a condensing lens to see the

41
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.452
funders with a BIO.

42
00:01:47.452 --> 00:01:49.420
There are different powers of lenses

43
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.860
with the most commonly used, 20 doctors, 28 or 30.

44
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.300
Different lenses create different fields of view and consequently different

45
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.555
degrees of magnification as illustrated here.

46
00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:06.100
Like with any patient examination,

47
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.159
ensure you wash your hands and are wearing appropriate PPE.

48
00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:11.300
Explain to the patient that you'll be

49
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.220
examining the back of the eye and that the bright light may be uncomfortable.

50
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.340
Make sure that they're

51
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.740
happy to proceed and answer any questions they may have.

52
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.780
Dialate the patient's pupils to enable

53
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.928
a full funders view.

54
00:02:22.928 --> 00:02:26.660
A topical anesthetic can also be used to ensure the patient is comfortable

55
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:27.644
during the examination.

56
00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:29.940
The patient should be examined ideally lying

57
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.020
supine or in a reclining chair.

58
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.140
Examining a baby may be challenging and you should

59
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.980
consider using old sucrose before the examination, swaddling the child, instilling

60
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:44.020
local anesthetic drops or using a speculum to facilitate a thorough examination.

61
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.820
When the patient is dilated, the room should be dimly lit to maximize contrast.

62
00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.385
For this video we'll be keeping the lights on.

63
00:02:51.385 --> 00:02:53.380
You should examine the patient in a

64
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.598
quite environment to allow a comfortable examination.

65
00:02:55.598 --> 00:02:57.500
Stand at the head of the

66
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.540
patient, adjust the bed or chair height to ensure both you and the patient are

67
00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:01.950
comfortable.

68
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.460
Angle the light source towards the patient's eye and examine the

69
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.324
red reflex.

70
00:03:07.324 --> 00:03:12.508
Then use a condensing lens to bring the posterior pole into view.

71
00:03:12.508 --> 00:03:12.940
You

72
00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.429
can gently arrest your hand on the patient's forehead for stability.

73
00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.380
Ensure that the

74
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.028
patient's eye is in line with your light source and condensing lens.

75
00:03:21.028 --> 00:03:21.940
You will have

76
00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.803
to move the lens to bring the funders into focus.

77
00:03:24.803 --> 00:03:27.380
It takes practice to keep the funders in view

78
00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.295
and to become comfortable finding the working distance of the lens.

79
00:03:30.295 --> 00:03:31.620
You should be aware that

80
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.580
different lenses and patients will have different working distances.

81
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.780
It's important to have the

82
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.885
BIO condensing lens and the area of retina to be examined all aligned.

83
00:03:41.885 --> 00:03:43.780
If you see shadows on the

84
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.758
edge of your view, it may mean that one of these are incorrectly aligned.

85
00:03:48.758 --> 00:03:50.180
To avoid unwanted glare,

86
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.140
you can try tilting the lens as this may help to eliminate this.

87
00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.751
Bring the posterior pole into view.

88
00:03:56.751 --> 00:03:58.900
You should examine the optic disc and the

89
00:03:58.900 --> 00:03:59.282
macular.

90
00:03:59.282 --> 00:04:03.860
Adjust the brightness according to the quality of view and patient comfort.

91
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.620
On initial examination the lighter will be very bright for the patient and this

92
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.020
will settle once the retina is bleached in all four quadrants.

93
00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:16.340
To view the peripheral retina, change the angulation by asking the patient to

94
00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.022
look in the direction of the area to be examined.

95
00:04:19.022 --> 00:04:20.900
You can move around the bed and

96
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.506
tilt your body to view the peripheral fundus.

97
00:04:23.506 --> 00:04:25.460
To see the superior and inferior

98
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.940
fundus, you may find it easier to stand at the temporal side of the patient as

99
00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:29.683
soon here.

100
00:04:29.683 --> 00:04:31.540
Again, this skill takes practice.

101
00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.140
You may find you keep losing your

102
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:34.477
view.

103
00:04:34.477 --> 00:04:38.860
This is likely due to incorrect alignment of the BIO condensing lens and

104
00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:39.912
the peripheral fundus.

105
00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:43.420
If this happens, try to adjust the angle of examination

106
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.320
and ensure that you are in line correctly.

107
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.760
You should examine all quadrants of the fundus.

108
00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.800
You should feel comfortable moving around the bed

109
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.360
and tilting your body to help your view.

110
00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.200
You will likely need to keep the eyelid open

111
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.040
to maintain your view with your fingers.

112
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.680
If the patient struggles to maintain their direction of gaze,

113
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.240
you can use a fixation target

114
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:09.750
such as the patient's finger or thumb as shown here.

115
00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.110
With slow indentation, the BIO can allow visualization

116
00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.310
of the far periphery of the retina.

117
00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.550
This technique brings the anterior retina into view.

118
00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.750
It can be helpful to view the aurisorata

119
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.710
and look for peripheral retinal pathology,

120
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.630
such as anterior retinal tears,

121
00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.790
that would otherwise be not visible on a regular examination.

122
00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.110
You will require appropriate anesthetic drops

123
00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.150
and an indenting instrument.

124
00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.510
You should warn the patient that this may be uncomfortable.

125
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.550
To perform skeletal indentation,

126
00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.190
position the patient as before.

127
00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.510
You can indent directly onto the sclera or over the eyelids.

128
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:47.397
Ask the patient to look in the opposite area to be examined, allow the

129
00:05:47.397 --> 00:05:48.230
indenter to rest

130
00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:53.150
tangentially against the area to be indented, then ask the patient to look towards the area

131
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.390
to be examined and exert a gentle pressure.

132
00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:00.510
This example shows the examination of the superior retina being examined.

133
00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.550
The patient is asked to look downwards towards their feet.

134
00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:08.110
The indenter is then placed on the superior eyelid and rolled.

135
00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:11.550
Then the patient is asked to look upwards.

136
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.417
a gentle pressure is required.

137
00:06:13.417 --> 00:06:16.030
This is a dynamic examination and the

138
00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:18.990
indentor will be moved to observe the peripheral retina.

139
00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.110
You should thank the patient and give them ample time to recover from the

140
00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.977
examination before they send up.

141
00:06:23.977 --> 00:06:26.590
Document your findings and inform the patient

142
00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.954
of your conclusion if appropriate.

143
00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:31.790
This completes the BIO fundus examination.

144
00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:35.470
It's a challenging practical skill but with practice you will develop muscle

145
00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.270
memory to help you maintain a clear view of the fundus.

146
00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:41.230
We hope you found this video helpful, thank you very much for watching.
